                 <h2>Short URLs</h2>
                 <p><a href="https://blog.tr.im/post/159369789/tr-im-r-i-p">News like this</a> only makes clearer what <a href="https://joshua.schachter.org/2009/04/on-url-shorteners.html">others</a> have already noted: URL shortening services aren&#8217;t good for the web.</p>

<p>But short URLs have their uses, notably in print and on Twitter. This site will be on Twitter shortly, and I hope to make a print version (details still being sorted out) of the Chronicle available someday, so: The Baseball Chronicle now offers its own short URLs.</p>

<p>As long as the site exists, these URLs will too. No link rot, no obfuscation&#8212;just handy little URLs. You can find them in the sidebar, next to every story on the site.</p>

<p>Here&#8217;s an example: <a href="https://bbchron.com/go/129">https://bbchron.com/go/129</a> sends you to <a href="https://thebaseballchronicle.com/personal_stories/50_years_a_cubs_fan_part_one">https://thebaseballchronicle.com/personal_stories/50_years_a_cubs_fan_part_one</a></p>

<p>Enjoy, and happy linking.</p>

                 <p class="dateitalic">&mdash;August 12, 2009</p>
